    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Religious conservatives ground their opposition in the metaphysical doctrine of *imago Dei* (the image of God), arguing that because every human life is a direct creation of the Divine, any act of ending it—even out of mercy—is a usurpation of God's exclusive right to life and death, effectively treating the patient as an object rather than a sacred subject. In contrast, religious liberals construct their case through a sophisticated re-interpretation of *agape* (compassion) and the concept of personhood, asserting that when the body becomes a source of unbearable pain, the soul's dignity is violated by forced suffering, and thus the right to self-determination is the highest expression of love and respect for the individual's unique journey. While conservatives view the law as a violation of ontological truth, liberals view it as a necessary evolution of theological ethics to prioritize the lived reality of suffering over abstract dogma.",
      "source": "target",
